  "articleBody": "Picking the right trim for a finished basement isn't quite the same as choosing molding for an upstairs bedroom. Below-grade spaces naturally experience shifting humidity and occasional moisture from concrete slabs, which means traditional materials might swell, warp, or invite mold if installed improperly.\n\nWhen planning your basement finish carpentry, material stability should lead your decision. Standard medium-density fiberboard (MDF) absorbs water quickly and tends to puff up irreversibly if basement humidity spikes or if a minor spill occurs. For baseline durability, solid wood sealed on all faces or composite materials like PVC and polyurethane provide reliable performance. PVC trim won't rot or support fungal growth even if moisture comes into contact with the bottom edge.\n\nWhat Can Change the Right Choice:\n- Basement moisture history: If dampness lingers, lean toward non-porous composites rather than standard wood.\n- Wall and floor alignment: Irregular concrete or framing requires flexible profiles or skilled scribing to sit flush.\n- Fastening backing: Trims must anchor into structural wall studs, not just drywall gypsum core.\n\nWhen you're ready to update your space with durable, clean interior trim, call Abraham Becerra at HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866 to review options for your basement project.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
